        "officialText": "OBSERVATION IN SECURITY:\nSecurity officers are usually the first people on a crime scene and must observe details accurately without overlooking evidence.\n\nTHE 5 HUMAN SENSES USED IN OBSERVATION:\n1. Sight: Seeing intruders, damaged fences, smoke, suspicious vehicles.\n2. Hearing: Listening for breaking glass, footsteps, strange engines, cries for help.\n3. Smell: Detecting smoke, burning electrical wires, gas leaks, alcohol, narcotics.\n4. Touch: Feeling overheated machinery, freshly cut fence wire, vibrations.\n5. Taste: (Used with extreme caution) Noticing chemical contamination in water or air.\n\nOBSERVATIONAL PATTERN FOR DESCRIBING PERSONS:\nA security officer must describe suspects using a systematic order:\n\u2022 Gender (Sex): Male or female.\n\u2022 Race / Complexion: Light, dark, medium.\n\u2022 Age: Estimated age range.\n\u2022 Height & Build: Tall, short, slender, muscular, heavy-set.\n\u2022 Hair & Face: Length, color, facial hair, scars, tattoos.\n\u2022 Clothing: Headgear, jacket, shirt, trousers, footwear.\n\u2022 Peculiarities: Limp, twitch, accent, jewelry.\n\nOBSERVATIONAL PATTERN FOR VEHICLES:\nColor, Make, Model, Registration number (number plate), and distinctive damage or stickers.",
