        "summary": "Powers of arrest under Section 42 of the Criminal Procedure Act 51/1977, Section 39 arrest procedures, and PSIRA rules.",
        "officialText": "POWERS OF ARREST (SECTION 42 CPA 51 OF 1977):\nA private citizen or security officer may arrest anyone without a warrant who:\n1. Commits or attempts to commit any Schedule 1 offence in his presence.\n2. He reasonably suspects of having committed a Schedule 1 offence.\n3. Is engaged in an affray (public fight).\n4. Is being pursued by someone authorized to arrest him.\n5. Is found at night in circumstances affording reasonable grounds for suspecting he has committed or is about to commit an offence.\n\nSCHEDULE 1 OFFENCES INCLUDE:\nTreasons, sedition, murder, culpable homicide, rape, robbery, assault with intent to do grievous bodily harm (GBH), arson, malicious injury to property, theft, breaking or entering premises, fraud, and kidnapping.\n\nPROCEDURE FOR EFFECTING ARREST (SECTION 39 CPA):\n1. Identify yourself clearly as a security officer.\n2. Inform the person that they are being placed under arrest.\n3. Inform the person of the reason for the arrest.\n4. Read the suspect their constitutional rights (right to remain silent, right to legal representation).\n5. Request the suspect to submit to custody. If necessary, touch the person's body or use minimum necessary force if they resist.\n6. Hand the suspect over to the SAPS as soon as reasonably possible without delay or unauthorized interrogation.\n\nPSIRA DISCIPLINARY OFFENCES:\nA registered security officer found guilty of improper conduct under PSIRA regulations may have their registration suspended, withdrawn, receive a fine, or be reprimanded.",
        "plainEnglish": "WHAT THIS MEANS IN EASY WORDS:\nIn South Africa, a security officer has the same power of arrest as any private citizen under Section 42 of the law.\nYou can arrest someone without a warrant if:\n\u2022 They commit a serious (Schedule 1) crime in front of your eyes (like stealing, beating someone badly, breaking a door, or robbery).\n\u2022 They are fighting in public.\nHow to arrest someone correctly:\n1. Say: 'I am Security Officer McMaster. You are under arrest for theft.'\n2. Say: 'You have the right to remain silent and anything you say can be used in court.'\n3. Don't beat the suspect! Only use the smallest force needed to stop them escaping.\n4. Hand them over to the SAPS immediately. Do not beat or torture them for a confession.",
