        "summary": "Two-way radio care, NATO phonetic alphabet, professional prowords (ROGER, OVER, OUT), and call signs.",
        "officialText": "TWO-WAY RADIO DISCIPLINE:\nRadios are expensive technical assets used for rapid command and emergency coordination.\n\nDO'S OF RADIO COMMUNICATION:\n\u2022 Keep radio room and control room strictly secured.\n\u2022 Use radios only for official security business.\n\u2022 Handle radios with extreme care and keep antenna intact.\n\u2022 Conduct radio checks before starting your shift (check battery, antenna, volume, squelch, channel).\n\u2022 Think before you speak: keep transmissions Brief, Accurate, Short, and Sharp (BASS).\n\nDON'TS OF RADIO COMMUNICATION:\n\u2022 Never use abusive, vulgar, or unprofessional language over the air.\n\u2022 Do not play music or allow background noise.\n\u2022 Do not clog up emergency airwaves with unnecessary chatter or private conversations.\n\u2022 Do not expose radios to direct rain, extreme sunlight, dust, or dropping.\n\nSTANDARD RADIO PROWORDS:\n\u2022 ROGER: Message received and understood.\n\u2022 OVER: My transmission has ended and I am expecting a reply from you.\n\u2022 OUT: My transmission has ended and no reply is expected / conversation finished.\n\u2022 SAY AGAIN: Please repeat your last message.\n\u2022 I SAY AGAIN: I am repeating my message.\n\u2022 SITREP: Situation Report.\n\u2022 MAYDAY: Life-threatening emergency distress call.\n\u2022 RELAY TO: Pass this message along to another designated station.\n\nPHONETIC ALPHABET (A\u2013Z):\nAlpha, Bravo, Charlie, Delta, Echo, Foxtrot, Golf, Hotel, India, Juliet, Kilo, Lima, Mike, November, Oscar, Papa, Quebec, Romeo, Sierra, Tango, Uniform, Victor, Whiskey, X-ray, Yankee, Zulu.",
