        "summary": "Internal vs external security threats, vulnerability assessments, and risk mitigation strategies.",
        "officialText": "DEFINITION OF A THREAT:\nA threat is any action, event, or condition tending to be detrimental to an organization, its personnel, or assets, intensified by time, location, and the nature of the operations.\n\nINTERNAL THREATS (FROM WITHIN THE WORKFORCE):\n\u2022 Theft of stock, inventory, and copper cables.\n\u2022 Fraud, invoice falsification, and embezzlement.\n\u2022 Sabotage of manufacturing equipment during industrial strikes.\n\u2022 Bribery and collusion with external criminal syndicates.\n\u2022 Malicious injury to property.\n\nEXTERNAL THREATS (FROM OUTSIDE SYNDICATES):\n\u2022 Armed robbery and cash-in-transit heists.\n\u2022 Hijacking of delivery trucks.\n\u2022 Burglary and forced break-ins.\n\u2022 Kidnapping, assault, extortion, and cyber intrusions.\n\nRISK MITIGATION:\nLayered security ('Defense in Depth'): Combining physical barriers, electronic surveillance, strict access screening, and intelligence gathering to deter, detect, delay, and respond to threats.",
